1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,268
A city can depend on one bridge, one road, or one rail connection.

2
00:00:06,268 --> 00:00:17,446
When that link fails, the question reaches beyond a line on the map. The effects can
travel further. One local failure can have system-wide consequences.

3
00:00:17,446 --> 00:00:25,000
How does the entire network change, and who loses access? That is the question behind
ResiliRoute.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,786
A single closure can push trips onto other corridors. A road that was lightly used can
become a bottleneck.

5
00:00:33,786 --> 00:00:42,786
Some journeys become longer. Others become impossible within the modeled network. Access
to hospitals and essential services can become uneven.

6
00:00:42,786 --> 00:00:49,643
A normal map shows the missing road, but makes these cascading consequences difficult to
see.

7
00:00:49,643 --> 00:00:55,000
That makes communicating infrastructure vulnerability a challenge, especially for
essential-service access.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,829
I built ResiliRoute, an urban mobility resilience simulator, to explore those
consequences.

9
00:01:00,829 --> 00:01:08,143
It calculates changes in travel time, access, congestion, efficiency, and resilience
when transport links become unavailable.

10
00:01:08,143 --> 00:01:11,171
Harbor City provides a controlled demonstration.

11
00:01:11,171 --> 00:01:20,000
Real City Lab extends the same simulation engine to real infrastructure, using
OpenStreetMap. Routes and demand are recalculated, together.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,357
Here, I select a small area of Okayama in Japan. Both riverbanks are included.

13
00:01:27,357 --> 00:01:36,000
Users can draw two corners on the map, adjust the bounds, or start from an example area.

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,786
Build Mobility Network retrieves roads and facilities through the server. Geometry is
preserved along each road.

15
00:01:43,786 --> 00:01:52,000
This area produces six hundred eighty-seven intersections, nine hundred eighty-three
road segments, and eighteen modeled residential origins.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,786
Now I select this real river crossing and simulate its closure. Closed links carry zero
modeled flow.

17
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:08,000
Average modeled travel time rises from two point eight to three point four minutes.
Resilience falls to ninety-one.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,429
The same hospital journey now takes seven point two minutes instead of four point four.

19
00:02:16,429 --> 00:02:24,000
Traffic has moved to alternative corridors, creating consequences well beyond the closed
bridge.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,929
One alternative road carries about three thousand modeled trips per hour, up from
roughly six hundred.

21
00:02:31,929 --> 00:02:36,000
Overall averages can hide this concentrated pressure.

22
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:44,214
The stress test removes candidate roads individually and recalculates their additional
impact. Scores measure consequences, not failure probabilities.

23
00:02:44,214 --> 00:02:52,000
For this larger network, thirty-five candidates are screened, rather than claiming an
exhaustive ranking of every road.

24
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,732
After a second closure, essential-service access falls to ninety-five point nine
percent.

25
00:02:57,732 --> 00:03:04,982
Recovery analysis compares reopening each road independently. These gains are
comparisons, not a combined repair plan.

26
00:03:04,982 --> 00:03:12,000
The leading restoration recovers seventeen point four resilience points, and four point
one access points.

27
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:21,321
Roads become graph edges; intersections become nodes. Dijkstra routing finds paths, and
modeled travel demand is assigned over twenty-four iterations.

28
00:03:21,321 --> 00:03:27,821
A bounded volume-capacity function adjusts travel costs. Closed roads are excluded from
every route.

29
00:03:27,821 --> 00:03:35,000
Automated tests cover routing, imports, closures, recovery, and bilingual interactions.
All metric definitions are documented.

30
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,900
The analyst converts numerical evidence into a planning brief. This recording uses local
analysis.

31
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:47,643
An optional server-side language model explains the same results, with automatic
fallback.

32
00:03:47,643 --> 00:03:53,814
Geometry and facilities come from OpenStreetMap. Capacity, demand, and some speeds are
modeled.

33
00:03:53,814 --> 00:04:02,000
Assumptions are visible: this is educational what-if analysis, with no disaster
prediction. Outside-area routes are excluded too.

34
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,750
Potential users include planners, local governments, researchers, educators, and
preparedness organizations.

35
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:14,869
Real City Lab makes the project reusable: each small area becomes a new, reproducible
analysis.

36
00:04:14,869 --> 00:04:20,274
My next step is educational workshops and feedback from planning professionals.

37
00:04:20,274 --> 00:04:27,393
A future model could offer free education, professional analysis, and support for custom
organizational data.

38
00:04:27,393 --> 00:04:37,000
These are proposed directions, before customer validation. Before expanding, I would
validate model usefulness, data quality, and repeated workshop use.

39
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,768
Cities cannot prevent every disruption. They can understand which connections matter and
where access is vulnerable.

40
00:04:44,768 --> 00:04:49,232
ResiliRoute makes those consequences visible before a disruption happens.

41
00:04:49,232 --> 00:04:54,000
Select a city. Break a link. Understand the impact.
